PathScale EKOPath / Path64 compiler suite — IRIX fork
=====================================================

What this is
------------

Path64 is the open-source release of the PathScale EKOPath compiler suite:
optimizing C, C++ and Fortran compilers. It descends from Open64, which in
turn descends from SGI's Pro64/MIPSpro compilers. The compilers share one
intermediate representation (WHIRL) and one back end: interprocedural
analysis (IPA), the loop nest optimizer (LNO), the global optimizer (WOPT)
and code generation (CG). PathScale's releases targeted x86-64; the MIPS
back end was maintained for SiCortex's MIPS64 Linux machines.

This repository holds the whole suite in one tree:

    compiler/compiler      driver, front ends, optimizers, code generators
    compiler/assembler     PathAS assembler (x86-64)
    compiler/compiler-rt   low-level runtime routines
    compiler/libcxxrt      C++ ABI runtime
    compiler/libunwind     unwinder
    compiler/libdwarf-bsd  DWARF library used by the back end
    compiler/stdcxx        C++ standard library
    compiler/pathdb        debugger

The top-level CMakeLists.txt builds them together.


This fork: IRIX support
-----------------------

This fork is adding IRIX 6.5 as a target: MIPS, the n32 ABI, the R10000
(MIPS IV) and big-endian code, cross-compiled from Linux.

Working now:

  * The C compiler builds for mips64-sgi-irix6.5 with modern GCC/Clang
    and CMake 3.x.
  * pathcc -n32 -mips4 -march=r10000 compiles C to R10000 n32 code at
    -O0 to -O3, using MIPS IV instructions only.
  * pathcc assembles and links with GNU binutils for IRIX, producing
    n32 executables that request /lib32/rld and use IRIX's libc and
    libm from a sysroot.
  * The runtime libraries (libgcc from compiler-rt, libeh from
    libunwind, libmv, libmpath, libpscrt) build for IRIX with MIPS IV
    code. pathcc links them statically, so executables need only IRIX's
    own shared libraries.
  * libeh (libunwind) finds unwind tables through rld's _RLD_DLADDR
    service and the PT_GNU_EH_FRAME segment that ld --eh-frame-hdr
    adds, and resumes into landing pads on n32. The compiler emits
    call-frame information (.cfi_*) for MIPS prologues.
  * -n32 defaults to -mips4 and the R10000 on IRIX.

Not done yet:

  * pathCC compiles C++ with exceptions for IRIX, and libcxxrt builds;
    a throw/catch program links (given a libstl). libeh does not yet
    restore floating-point callee-saved registers when it resumes.
  * The C++ standard library (stdcxx, libstl). Its configuration is
    normally produced by running test programs on the target.
  * __sync_val_compare_and_swap on MIPS returns 0/1 instead of the old
    value (__sync_bool_compare_and_swap is correct).
  * Fortran for IRIX, and the 64-bit ABI.
  * Testing on real IRIX hardware. The output has only been checked
    with binutils' readelf and objdump.
  * Using IRIX's own assembler (the output uses GNU as directives such
    as .cfi_*), and running the compiler natively on IRIX.


Requirements
------------

  * CMake 3.x. CMake 4 dropped support for the old cmake_minimum_required
    versions this tree uses.
  * Clang or GCC, plus Ninja (or make).
  * m4 (libdwarf generates sources with it) and static glibc (the pathcc
    driver links statically). On Fedora/RHEL: dnf install m4 glibc-static.

A Release build requires the PathScale compiler itself; build with
-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug when using GCC or Clang.


Building the IRIX cross compiler
--------------------------------

1. IRIX sysroot. Unpack the IRIX 6.5.30 headers and libraries (for
   example irix-root.6.5.30.tar.bz2) so that you have

       ~/devel/irix-sysroot/usr/include
       ~/devel/irix-sysroot/usr/lib32
       ~/devel/irix-sysroot/lib32/rld

2. GNU binutils for IRIX. Build binutils 2.23.2 with the SGUG RSE
   patches from https://github.com/sgidevnet/sgug-rse/tree/master/packages/binutils
   (binutils-2.22.52.0.4-no-config-h-check.patch,
   binutils2_23.sgifixes.patch, binutils.sgirelinkfix.patch,
   binutils.ldirixn32paths.patch, applied in that order with -p1).
   GNU as knows IRIX only as mips-sgi-irix6*, so configure with:

       CFLAGS="-O2 -std=gnu11 -fcommon -Wno-error \
               -Wno-implicit-function-declaration -Wno-int-conversion \
               -Wno-incompatible-pointer-types -Wno-implicit-int" \
       ../binutils-2.23.2/configure \
           --prefix=$HOME/devel/irix-toolchain \
           --target=mips-sgi-irix6.5 \
           --with-sysroot=$HOME/devel/irix-sysroot \
           --disable-werror --disable-nls --disable-gdb \
           --disable-sim --disable-gold
       make MAKEINFO=true && make MAKEINFO=true install

3. Configure and build the compiler:

       mkdir build-irix && cd build-irix
       S=$HOME/devel/irix-sysroot
       cmake -G Ninja .. \
           -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
           -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
           -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/devel/path64-irix \
           -DPSC_TARGET=mips64-sgi-irix6.5 \
           -DPATH64_ENABLE_TARGETS=mips_32 \
           -DPSC_TARGET_OS_mips_32=irix \
           -DPSC_CRT_PATH_mips_32=$S/usr/lib32/mips4 \
           -DPSC_CRTBEGIN_PATH_mips_32=$S/usr/lib32/mips4 \
           -DPSC_DYNAMIC_LINKER_mips_32=/lib32/rld \
           -DPSC_INCLUDE_PATH_mips_32=$S/usr/include \
           -DPSC_GCC_LIBRARY_DIRS=$S/usr/lib32/mips4 \
           -DPSC_BINUTILS_TARGET=mips-sgi-irix6.5 \
           -DPSC_BINUTILS_DIR=$HOME/devel/irix-toolchain/bin \
           -DPATH64_BUILD_STDCXX=OFF \
           -DPATH64_ENABLE_FORTRAN=OFF -DPATH64_ENABLE_MATHLIBS=OFF \
           -DPATH64_ENABLE_OPENMP=OFF -DPATH64_ENABLE_PROFILING=OFF \
           -DPATH64_ENABLE_TESTING=OFF \
           -DPATH64_ENABLE_PATHAS=OFF -DPATH64_ENABLE_DEFAULT_PATHAS=OFF
       ninja compiler-stage-C

   Large parallel builds have livelocked build hosts; -j8 has been fine
   on a 16-core, 30 GB machine.

   Then build the runtime libraries with the staged compiler:

       ninja compiler-stage-libs-C

4. Compile for IRIX with the staged driver:

       build-irix/Xcompiler/bin/pathcc -O2 -o hello hello.c

   -n32 -mips4 -march=r10000 are the IRIX defaults.

   Use -S to stop at R10000 assembly. The front end is GCC 4.2-based,
   so C99 code needs -std=c99.


Building for x86-64 Linux
-------------------------

The original native build:

    mkdir build && cd build
    cmake -G Ninja .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
        -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/prefix
    ninja

The initial-clone, update and install scripts date from when the
components lived in separate repositories. They are not needed: every
component is now in this tree.
